+        // regardless of connection/player state). This patch is confined to
+        // fpsgame/fps.cpp (Task 1 scope), so hooking client.cpp's network handlers or
+        // engine/octaedit.cpp's toggleedit() directly for the external-abort triggers is
+        // not an option - polling player1->state instead covers every one of ADR-0002's
+        // triggers exactly: a server-forced spawn, edit-mode entry (-> CS_EDITING) and
+        // spectator entry (-> CS_SPECTATOR) all move state away from CS_DEAD, so a single
+        // `state != CS_DEAD` check catches all three uniformly.
+        if(squadsess.state == SQUADSTATE_NONE) return;
+        if(!connected || intermission || !m_mp(gamemode) || player1->state != CS_DEAD)
+        {
+            squadexit(SQUADEND_ABORT);
+            return;
+        }
+        if(squadsess.state == SQUADSTATE_PENDING && lastmillis >= squadsess.start)
+        {
+            squadsess.state = SQUADSTATE_ACTIVE;
+            // amendment 5 (playtest 1 bug fix): snapshot camera1->yaw, NOT player1->yaw.
+            // Root cause (proved in the mirror harness, see fs-iter1-report.md): while
+            // player1->state==CS_DEAD, engine/rendergl.cpp's recomputecamera() detects
+            // game::detachcamera() (true whenever the hud player is CS_DEAD) and swaps
+            // camera1 to a separate tempcamera object, copied ONCE from player1 at the
+            // instant of death. From then on mousemove() mutates camera1->yaw/pitch
+            // directly every frame (live mouselook), but its player-sync line
+            // (`if(camera1!=player && !detachedcamera) player->yaw = camera1->yaw...`) is
+            // skipped because detachedcamera is now true - so player1->yaw/pitch are
+            // frozen at the exact moment-of-death value for the rest of the death, and
+            // camera1->yaw/pitch are the only thing that actually tracks the mouse. This
+            // is also exactly what engine/rendergl.cpp's squadview::start() reads
+            // (`camera.yaw = oldcamera->yaw` where `oldcamera = camera1`), so snapshotting
+            // camera1->yaw here keeps the target's placement basis and the rendered
+            // camera provably the same live source - not two paths that happen to agree.
+            squadfacingyaw = camera1->yaw;
+        }
